{"step_index":53,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-18T05:37:29Z","input_tokens":15354,"cache_read_tokens":166224,"output_tokens":3358,"thinking":"The audit report arrived, and it focuses on `@env-2`. Reviewing the report to address all critical and high-priority findings related to the environment. This should comprehensively address the verifier's concerns and align with the task's stated goals.\n\n\nReviewing the verification report's findings for `@env-2`. The first issue concerns track intrusion by hangars; the fix involves adjusting the hangar placement so the minimum distance to any track point is greater than 260m. The second finding addresses the `V5_HedgeRing`, which needs adjustment to maintain a distance greater than 300m from all track points. The final issue identified a bug in the albedo of grandstand flags, requiring code inspection.\n\n\nInvestigating the grandstand flags' albedo. The current implementation directly uses integer tuples (0-255) as color values in a linear color space, resulting in incorrect brightness. The fix requires dividing these values by 255.0 and applying an sRGB to linear conversion for accurate representation.\n\nExamining the shared tail-light material (`Mat_Neon_Orange`). This material is inappropriately used across multiple unrelated objects. A dedicated, single-user material (`Mat_FIA_RainLight`) will be created for the tail light, using an emission node with a specific color and strength, subject to a check in `v5_anim.py`.\n\n\nImplementing the rain light fix using a Principled BSDF with specific parameters. This ensures the rain light appears as a realistic glossy ruby housing when dim and flashes correctly with emission strength keyframes, without affecting other objects. The environment's visual clutter can be reduced by adjusting billboard placements near Stowe.\n\n\nInvestigating the bridge collision.  The bridge's position at `s = 2950m` caused conflicts with camera positions. Analysis indicates the cameras' positions at the collision points. Moving the bridge to `s = 3340.0m` with an adjusted offset should resolve the overlap. A raycast check will confirm the revised placement eliminates the collision with the three cameras.\n\nFurther, there is an issue with the brake point placement causing a camera blockage. The specific camera's side offset and the brake point's side placement are examined to understand the cause of the conflict.\n\n\nExamining the camera blockage issue, now focusing on the brake point placement. Brake boards will be set to an offset of `9.8m` at the hairpin. A raycast test will also be conducted on `Cam_S04_Hairpin`.\n\nInvestigating sky banding and aerial exposure issues. Observing that high-altitude shots are reading darker than track shots. Examining albedo values of asphalt and grass, specifically the impact of ground irradiance under sun and sky fill illumination, to understand their contribution to the brightness discrepancy.\n\n\nRaising the sky fill and slightly increasing the albedo of grass and asphalt, while keeping rubber lines and skidmarks dark, should improve the brightness of aerial shots and increase contrast with the track.\n\nThe vertex-interpolated sky shader will be replaced with a continuous per-pixel procedural shader to eliminate potential visual artifacts from discrete color segments.\n\n\nFocusing on the environment's organization and materials, beginning with the Outliner hygiene. The `RIG_F1_Race` object will be moved to a more appropriate collection, and all V5 meshes and lights will be relocated into specific sub-collections to declutter the root scene.\n\nInvestigating the brake rotor materials and glow, considering findings from the verification report. The node tree and existing animation drivers for `Mat_Brake_HeatGlow` will be examined to prevent unintended alterations or deletions. A brief test will be conducted to ensure animation integrity.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name"